# CLI reference

The `pontia` CLI configures the local installation and delegates durable control-plane operations to the running `pontiad` service.

Use `pontia COMMAND --help` for the exact options in your installed version.

## Setup and lifecycle

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `pontia init` | Select Agent Clients, create persistent configuration, install integrations, start the per-user service, and offer remote setup |
| `pontia setup [CLIENT]` | Install or repair recommended local integrations; select Clients when omitted; enable the selected Clients |
| `pontia up` | Install or refresh the per-user service definition and start or restart the service |
| `pontia status` | Show the data directory, local service health, signed-in user, remote handle, and selected edge |
| `pontia doctor` | Explain service, configuration, login, and remote-registration checks, with repair hints |
| `pontia down` | Stop and disable the per-user service |
| `pontia update` | Install the latest signed stable `pontia` and `pontiad` release |
| `pontia uninstall` | Remove Pontia integrations, shell configuration, and autostart services |
| `pontia --version` | Print the installed version |
| `pontia completions SHELL` | Print the completion script for a shell |

If Pontia-started agents are running, `pontia down` and `pontia uninstall` list their Sessions and require `--force` to stop them before continuing. This check also works when the service is offline. With `--force`, an older Codex background server whose ownership cannot be verified is left running with a warning.

`pontia uninstall` shows the cleanup list and asks for confirmation; use `--yes` to skip the prompt. It keeps the binaries, configuration, credentials, history, cloud registration, and Agent Client installations. Open a new terminal after cleanup. Run `pontia init` to set up again.

`pontia setup` accepts `pi`, `codex`, or `claude` and applies the recommended configuration without additional Client options.

See [Initialization](/docs/initialization) for interactive setup and the equivalent step-by-step command sequence.

Stopping `pontiad` stops observation, input reception, and Workflow scheduling. Agent Clients manage the lifecycle of their native conversations separately.

## Set a Bot description

From the current main Bot Session, run `pontia bot set-description --description TEXT` to fill in an empty description. The command accepts up to 2,000 characters. Users can edit or clear the description in Bot settings.

## Bot helper compaction

Run `pontia bot helper compact --session-id ID` from the current Bot Session to request context compaction for one of its helpers. The helper must be idle or interrupted, with manual compaction available from its client. Wait for a working helper to become idle before retrying.

`pontia bot helper check --session-id ID` shows context usage and `capabilities.manual_compaction`. Add `--json` to either command for JSON output. A successful compact result contains `accepted: true` and an `operation_id`; it confirms the request, not completion. If the result is unknown, inspect the helper before retrying because compaction may already have started.

## Shell completion

`pontia completions` supports `bash`, `zsh`, `fish`, `elvish`, and `powershell`. Add the line for your shell to its configuration file, then open a new terminal:

```sh
# bash: ~/.bashrc
source <(pontia completions bash)

# zsh: ~/.zshrc, after compinit
source <(pontia completions zsh)

# fish: run once
mkdir -p ~/.config/fish/completions
pontia completions fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/pontia.fish

# elvish: ~/.config/elvish/rc.elv
eval (pontia completions elvish | slurp)
```

```powershell
# PowerShell: $PROFILE
pontia completions powershell | Out-String | Invoke-Expression
```

## Agent Clients

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `pontia client list` | List every Agent Client entry with its short ID, name, type, and enabled state |
| `pontia client list --json` | List every entry as JSON, including its ID |
| `pontia client show CLIENT` | Show one entry |
| `pontia client enable CLIENT` | Allow new Sessions to use the entry |
| `pontia client disable CLIENT` | Stop new Sessions from using the entry; existing Sessions are unchanged |
| `pontia client reset CLIENT` | Delete the entry's saved settings, which also disables it |

`CLIENT` is a short ID such as `pi`, `codex`, `claude-agent`, or `grok`. Use `--id ID` with an ID from `pontia client list --json` when a short ID is ambiguous.

## Agent tools

`pontia --help` lists `workflow` and `bot` under Agent tools.

### Workflows

| Command | Context | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pontia workflow run FILE` | Any local shell | Parse a TOML definition and start a Workflow through the local API |
| `pontia workflow show [WORKFLOW_ID]` | Any local shell; ID optional inside a managed Node | Print compact Workflow, current Node, input, output, and Patch context |
| `pontia workflow submit` | Current managed Agent Node pane | Declare the Node's output ready |
| `pontia workflow patch request` | Current managed Agent Node pane | Report a blocking problem and start replanning |
| `pontia workflow patch apply` | Current Re-planner pane | Accept the candidate definition as a new revision |
| `pontia workflow patch block` | Current Re-planner pane | Finish the Patch without applying the candidate definition |

Run pane-scoped commands in the current Agent Node or Re-planner's Pontia-managed `tmux` pane.

## Cloud login and remote access

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `pontia login` | Complete browser-based device authorization and store a CLI credential |
| `pontia remote edges` | List available edges as a JSON array of IDs and names |
| `pontia remote enable` | Register this device, select an edge interactively, and create its E2E identity |
| `pontia remote enable --edge EDGE_ID --name NAME` | Register without prompts; both options are required |
| `pontia remote edit` | Edit the name, handle, and Edge interactively; Enter keeps each current value |
| `pontia remote edit --name NAME` | Change the device display name |
| `pontia remote edit --handle HANDLE` | Change the device Dashboard URL handle |
| `pontia remote rotate-key` | Replace the device E2E identity and key version |
| `pontia remote disable` | Unregister this device and remove its local remote identity |

Remote configuration changes that affect `pontiad` restart a running managed service. See [switching Edge](/docs/device-management#switch-edge) for stopped services and recovery.

## Exit status and errors

`pontia status` and `pontia doctor` exit successfully when the local service is running and its HTTP health check passes. Read the account and remote results separately; cloud registration does not prove that the device is reachable from the internet.

Use the exit status to check command success. Errors are written to standard error with a `pontia:` prefix. Include the redacted error when diagnosing a failure.

## `pontia-edge`

A self-hosted Edge has a separate root-operated CLI:

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `pontia-edge init --edge-id ID --ticket TICKET` | Initialize and register an Edge; normally use the full Cloud-generated command |
| `pontia-edge retry` | Resume incomplete initialization on the same server |
| `pontia-edge update` | Install the latest signed stable Edge release |
| `pontia-edge auto-update enable` | Install and enable update checks every 10 minutes |
| `pontia-edge auto-update disable` | Disable future checks |
| `pontia-edge auto-update status` | Show timer schedule and the last update result |

See [Self-host an Edge](/docs/self-hosted-edge) before using these commands directly.
